{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-331463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=3-5{e}^{2t}$ for $s>2.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-331463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{3-5{e}^{2t}} & =L{3}+L{-5{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =3L{1}-5L{{e}^{2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k\n$$\n\n$$\nL{1}=\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{{e}^{2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-2},\\,s>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =3L{1}-5L{{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{s}-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{5}{s-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(s-2)-5s}{s(s-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(s+3)}{s(s-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{1}$ is defined for $s\\in (0,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{2t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (2,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{f(t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(0,\\infty )\\cap (2,\\infty )=(2,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-331463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4575261e49ce28ccfc3540c372e6382d7c68a7f7e64c7164d3b331ee91b19d36"} {"question" "q-331463" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-331463" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{5}{s-2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-331464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=1-3{e}^{4t}$ for $s>4.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-331464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1-3{e}^{4t}} & =L{1}+L{-3{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =L{1}-3L{{e}^{4t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k\n$$\n\n$$\nL{1}=\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{{e}^{4t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-4},\\,s>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{1}-3L{{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{s}-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{3}{s-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-4)-3s}{s(s-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(s+2)}{s(s-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{1}$ is defined for $s\\in (0,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{5t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (5,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{f(t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(0,\\infty )\\cap (4,\\infty )=(4,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-331464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4575261e49ce28ccfc3540c372e6382d7c68a7f7e64c7164d3b331ee91b19d36"} {"question" "q-331464" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-331464" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{3}{s-4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-331458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{5t}-{e}^{2t}$ for $s>5.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-331458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{5t}-{e}^{2t}} & =L{{e}^{5t}}+L{-{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{5t}}-L{{e}^{2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{5t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-5},\\,s>5 \\\\ L{{e}^{2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-2},\\,s>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{5t}}-L{{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-5}-\\frac{1}{s-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-2)-(s-5)}{(s-5)(s-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{s-2-s+5}{(s-5)(s-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(s-5)(s-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{5t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (5,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{2t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (2,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{f(t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(5,\\infty )\\cap (2,\\infty )=(5,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-331458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4575261e49ce28ccfc3540c372e6382d7c68a7f7e64c7164d3b331ee91b19d36"} {"question" "q-331458" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-331458" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{s-5}-\\frac{1}{s-2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-89589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-2t}-{e}^{4t}$ for $s>4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-89589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-2t}-{e}^{4t}} & =L{{e}^{-2t}}+L{-{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{-2t}}-L{{e}^{4t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+2},\\,s>-2 \\\\ L{{e}^{4t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-4},\\,s>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{-2t}}-L{{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}-\\frac{1}{s-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-4)-(s+2)}{(s+2)(s-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6}{(s+2)(s-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{(s+2)(s-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{-2t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-2,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{4t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (4,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{f(t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(-2,\\infty )\\cap (4,\\infty )=(4,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-89589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4575261e49ce28ccfc3540c372e6382d7c68a7f7e64c7164d3b331ee91b19d36"} {"question" "q-89589" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-89589" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2}{(s-2)(s+4)}"] ["math" "-\\frac{6}{(s+2)(s-4)}"] ["math" "\\frac{2s-6}{(s+2)(s-4)}"] ["math" "\\frac{2s}{(s+2)(s-4)}"] ["math" "\\frac{s+4}{(s-2)(s+4)}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-89589" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{6}{(s+2)(s-4)}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-331459" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-3t}-{e}^{t}$ for $s>1.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-331459" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-3t}-{e}^{t}} & =L{{e}^{-3t}}+L{-{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{-3t}}-L{{e}^{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+3},\\,s>-3 \\\\ L{{e}^{t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-1},\\,s>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{-3t}}-L{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+3}-\\frac{1}{s-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-1)-(s+3)}{(s+3)(s-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{(s+3)(s-1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{(s+3)(s-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{-3t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-3,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (1,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{f(t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(-3,\\infty )\\cap (1,\\infty )=(1,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-331459" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4575261e49ce28ccfc3540c372e6382d7c68a7f7e64c7164d3b331ee91b19d36"} {"question" "q-331459" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-331459" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{s+3}-\\frac{1}{s-1}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-341109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{kt}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-341109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that, by definition\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)=\\frac{{e}^{4t}+{e}^{-4t}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{4t}+{e}^{-4t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{4t}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{-4t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{4t}}{2}}+L{\\frac{{e}^{-4t}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{4t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-4t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{4t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-4},\\,s>4 \\\\ L{{e}^{-4t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+4},\\,s>-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{4t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-4t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{1}{s+4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{(s+4)+(s-4)}{(s-4)(s+4)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}-16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{4t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (4,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{-4t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-4,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(4,\\infty )\\cap (-4,\\infty )=(4,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-341109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/activity-meta