"/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-341107" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{kt}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-341107" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that, by definition\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)=\\frac{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}}+L{\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{2t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-2},\\,s>2 \\\\ L{{e}^{-2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+2},\\,s>-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{2t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{s-2}+\\frac{1}{s+2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{(s+2)+(s-2)}{(s-2)(s+2)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}-4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{2t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (2,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{-2t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-2,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(2,\\infty )\\cap (-2,\\infty )=(2,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-341107" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4575261e49ce28ccfc3540c372e6382d7c68a7f7e64c7164d3b331ee91b19d36"} {"question" "q-341107" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-341107" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}-4}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}-4}"] ["math" "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}"] ["math" "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-341107" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-341107" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-341107" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "s>-2"] ["math" "s>0"] ["math" "s>2"] ["math" "s\\ge -2"] ["math" "s\\ge 0"] ["math" "s\\ge 2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-341107" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "s>2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-341190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that for $s>\\omega ,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the Laplace transform of $f(t)=4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(8t)+3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)$ for $s>8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-341190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(8t)+3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)} & =L{4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(8t)}+L{3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)} \\\\  & =4L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(8t)}+3L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\omega t)} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>\\omega , \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\omega t)} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>\\omega .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(8t)} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-64},\\,s>8 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)} & =\\frac{8}{{s}^{2}-64},\\,s>8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =4L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(8t)}+3L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-64}+3\\cdot \\frac{8}{{s}^{2}-64} \\\\  & =\\frac{4s}{{s}^{2}-64}+\\frac{24}{{s}^{2}-64} \\\\  & =\\frac{4s+24}{{s}^{2}-64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-341190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4575261e49ce28ccfc3540c372e6382d7c68a7f7e64c7164d3b331ee91b19d36"} {"question" "q-341190" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-f