t L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{6\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(10t)+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(10t)} & =L{6\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(10t)}+L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(10t)} \\\\  & =6L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(10t)}+L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(10t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\omega t)} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>\\omega , \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\omega t)} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>\\omega .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(10t)} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-100},\\,s>10 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(10t)} & =\\frac{10}{{s}^{2}-100},\\,s>10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =6L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(10t)}+L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(10t)} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-100}+\\frac{10}{{s}^{2}-100} \\\\  & =\\frac{6s}{{s}^{2}-100}+\\frac{10}{{s}^{2}-100} \\\\  & =\\frac{6s+10}{{s}^{2}-100}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-341192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4575261e49ce28ccfc3540c372e6382d7c68a7f7e64c7164d3b331ee91b19d36"} {"question" "q-341192" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14