the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{7\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t} & =L{7\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}+L{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t} \\\\  & =7L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}+4L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t} & =\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =7L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}+4L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t} \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}+4\\cdot \\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{7s}{{s}^{2}+9}+\\frac{12}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{7s+12}{{s}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-331504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4575261e49ce28ccfc3540c372e6382d7c68a7f7e64c7164d3b331ee91b19d36"} {"question" "q-331504" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-331504" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7s+12}{s^2+9}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-331500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the Laplace transform of $f(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t$ for $s>0.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-331500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t} & =L{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}+L{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t} \\\\  & =5L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}-2L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t} & =\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ 