per/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-341191" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)+6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t)} & =L{2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)}+L{6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t)} \\\\  & =2L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)}+6L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\omega t)} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>\\omega , \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\omega t)} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>\\omega .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-81},\\,s>9 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t)} & =\\frac{9}{{s}^{2}-81},\\,s>9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =2L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)}+6L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t)} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-81}+6\\cdot \\frac{9}{{s}^{2}-81} \\\\  & =\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}-81}+\\frac{54}{{s}^{2}-81} \\\\  & =\\frac{2s+54}{{s}^{2}-81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043226/state.json" "file_sha256" "89f9df8a11d6863461ca705818890b4a7d6909e6d9f6ddc5a4f2bade54fbb890"} {"question" "q-341191" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/referen